import{_ as i,P as n,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.21 Law, Authorization, Procedure, and Responsibility","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.21 Law, Authorization, Procedure, and Responsibility","xkChars":0,"xkWords":477,"xkLessons":{"D.21":{"title":"D.21 Law, Authorization, Procedure, and Responsibility","lead":"Verify legal texts, application to individual cases, powers, and actual remedies separately. Even a reliable total may be insufficient to support a decision concerning a legally critical time window.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5306–5321, original site location /theory/appendix-d/d-21#section-d-21. See the theory-v2 source for the current complete text; the historical research manuscript and excerpts remain available to download and consult in their original edition.","Brief quotation, translated from Chinese: ‘Correct arithmetic cannot compensate for a gap in legally critical timing information.’ This section first separates the validity of a legal source, applicability to a case, moral reasons, actual powers, remedial procedures, and realization in lived conditions. Whether a valid rule applies to this person and matter depends on conditions; legal existence does not end normative evaluation, and implementation does not prove its own legality. [T-main]","Natural law, legal positivism, legal realism, and other traditions serve here as research entry points, not as positions compressed into a single answer. Institutional texts supply evidence about rules, but cannot alone prove compliance in practice. Observed unauthorized conduct must still be recorded as fact: its invalidity does not erase the effects it has already produced. [T-main]","Consent, authorization, agency, representation, and standing respectively concern the person's willingness, the source of power, the scope of acting for another, the representative relationship, and procedural status. Some public procedures are not based on private delegation by each individual; that does not mean everyone consents. Nor can personal consent supply public powers that an institution lacks. Record a model's recommendation, actual implementation, and subsequent confirmation separately. [T-main]","Composite procedures can lawfully connect powers; separate verification does not mean procedures can never connect. What matters is that the source, scope, and conditions of authority hold at every node. Chaining several invalid fragments cannot create valid authorization, and authority at one node does not automatically cover later dispositions. [T-main]","Hearing views, respecting procedure, changing evidence, changing a decision, paying compensation, and changing rules may occur at different times. A decision remaining unchanged cannot by itself establish that views went unheard; being heard does not prove that the decision is correct. Relief in an individual case does not establish that every institutional problem has been resolved. [T-main]","Punishment, prevention, compensation, truth, nonviolent coexistence, and forgiveness have different objects and aims; one cannot offset another. T0 prohibits forced reconciliation without rejecting all lawful evidence gathering. Explaining how organizational conditions caused harm does not automatically exempt individuals from responsibility; identifying an individual's error does not erase the roles of upstream design, resources, and management. [T-main]","The source's annual-income teaching example separates the reliability of data from the sufficiency of its transformation. A reliable annual total can still correspond to many short-term income paths. If benefit rules hinge on a short-term window, directly averaging the total introduces an additional assumption. Who must supply further evidence, how absence is handled, and whether enforcement is suspended must follow the specific rules, rather than authorization invented by the model. [T-main]","Historical reference: paragraph [2] of the Federal Court of Australia's June 11, 2021 Prygodicz judgment describes averaging ATO income into fortnights and comparing it with reported income; [5] explains that income may be intermittent and variable. Item 1 of Annexure C explicitly lists the joint conditions under which decisions were invalid, including sole reliance on ATO data without evidence of constant fortnightly income or other supporting evidence. This is not a generalization that ‘all averaging is unlawful.’ [P-FCA]","Paragraph [65] records admissions in the Commonwealth's defence of July 17, 2020; their status must be distinguished from the court's declarations in the annexure. This case is used only to show that reliable aggregate data do not automatically supply missing temporal evidence in an individual case. The judgment is not taken to establish that no party had any real debt, nor that all refunds, freely accepted receipt, or institutional repairs were complete. [P-FCA]","When the error lies in a temporal transformation, correct the inference and derived decisions; reliable source records need not necessarily be deleted. Explanation, correction of source data, notification, changing a decision, compensation, and the person's freely accepted receipt are different endpoints and must be tracked separately. The additional distinction concerns the layer containing the error and whether the correction reaches the actual implementation node. [T-main]","Connect to §§5.5.5 and 5.5.7 for facts and norms, inference and responsibility; §11.3 for knowledge formation; §13.5 for action relationships; Part XIV for responsibility and protection; and §15.2 for closed-loop endpoints. These connections require evidence questions at each layer; a conceptual checklist is not a judicial decision. [T-main]","The eight entry points are: 21.01, the relationship between legal sources and normative evaluation; 21.02, whether the separation of powers and checks actually take effect; 21.03, the distinct statuses of rights, authorization, consent, and representation; 21.04, real entry points to evidence, procedure, and appeal; 21.05, the different aims of punishment, prevention, repair, and transition; 21.06, parallel inquiry into individual and organizational responsibility; 21.07, the application and conflict of multiple rule systems; and 21.08, whether digital decisions can be explained, corrected, and effectively appealed. This explains the navigation without claiming a complete reading of original works across all eight fields. [T-main]","For constitutive questions, first return to §2.4.3: whether a right, office, or decision has the relevant status requires checking applicable rules, valid sources, the acting subject, form, and application to the case. Realization, behavioral responses, and causal effects after that status is established require separate evidence. Legal validity, actual institutional existence, and normative legitimacy remain distinct. Failure to identify an effect cannot directly cancel a valid status, and an existing effect cannot manufacture lawful authorization."],"argumentSteps":["Identify the rule version, object, time window, and nodes of authority.","Separate original facts, transformation assumptions, and conclusions about the case, preserving reliable components.","Check the respective grounds for personal consent and public authority.","Record hearing, evidence correction, decision changes, compensation, and receipt separately.","Trace upstream conditions and individual actions without letting either exempt the other."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Validity of a legal source","leftText":"The rule's legal status.","rightTitle":"Application to a case","rightText":"Whether the facts concerning this person at this time satisfy the conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Consent","leftText":"The person's intention and its scope.","rightTitle":"Public authority","rightText":"The independent basis of power for an institution's actions."},{"leftTitle":"Correct annual total","leftText":"A reliable aggregate for the period.","rightTitle":"Correct fortnightly distribution","rightText":"Still requires evidence about allocation over time."},{"leftTitle":"Changing a decision","leftText":"A change in a procedural result.","rightTitle":"Realization of a remedy","rightText":"Notification, payment, and the person's receipt require separate checks."}],"boundaries":["Teaching counterexample from the source: annual total income is accurate, but the total cannot uniquely reconstruct income within short benefit windows.","Source counterexample: personal consent does not supply the public powers an institution requires.","Source boundary: an unchanged decision does not necessarily prove a failure to hear; relief in an individual case does not establish comprehensive institutional correction."],"questions":["Why can reliable data still lead to error?","Does lawful procedural continuity allow nodes to be skipped?","Can compensation demand forgiveness?","Must source data be deleted after an error is discovered?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/figures/d21-layers.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Income averaging and evidential limits in the 2021 Prygodicz judgment","href":"https://www.judgments.fedcourt.gov.au/judgments/Judgments/fca/single/2021/2021fca0634"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Income averaging and evidential limits in the 2021 Prygodicz judgment","text":"Paragraphs [2] and [5] describe fortnightly averaging and intermittent income; Annexure C 1(a)–(d) declares decisions invalid under the joint conditions it specifies, while [65] records admissions in the Commonwealth's defence.","sourceLabel":"Prygodicz v Commonwealth of Australia (No 2) [2021] FCA 634","sourceUrl":"https://www.judgments.fedcourt.gov.au/judgments/Judgments/fca/single/2021/2021fca0634","sourceLocator":"2021-06-11; reasons [2], [5], and [65], and orders in Annexure C, item 1(a)–(d)","boundary":"Limited to the specified case and conditions; this is not current legal advice and does not imply that all averaging is unlawful or that actual remedies are comprehensively complete.","href":"https://www.judgments.fedcourt.gov.au/judgments/Judgments/fca/single/2021/2021fca0634"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.21","start_line":5032,"end_line":5047,"startLine":5032,"endLine":5047}],"outputId":"section-d-21","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: the same total, different windows","text":"Two artificial income paths over two periods are A = (0, 1000) and B = (500, 500). Both total 1000 and average 500. Suppose a purely illustrative rule grants a benefit only when income in the current period does not exceed 400. A qualifies in the first period, while B qualifies in neither. Replacing A's first-period income with the average changes the judgment.","assumptions":"Two fictional periods, a threshold of 400, and no other eligibility conditions; these are not Australian statutory amounts or records of real parties.","boundary":"This shows that an aggregate does not uniquely determine window-specific values. It establishes no real debt conclusion or general prohibition on averaging."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample from the source: annual total income is accurate, but the total cannot uniquely reconstruct income within short benefit windows.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source counterexample: personal consent does not supply the public powers an institution requires.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Source boundary: an unchanged decision does not necessarily prove a failure to hear; relief in an individual case does not establish comprehensive institutional correction.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why can reliable data still lead to error?","answer":"A true total does not guarantee reconstruction of the distribution in critical windows; the transformation still requires evidence.","basis":"The information layer."},{"question":"Does lawful procedural continuity allow nodes to be skipped?","answer":"No. Every node must remain valid within its scope and conditions.","basis":"The authority layer."},{"question":"Can compensation demand forgiveness?","answer":"They have different aims; payment cannot be used to force reconciliation.","basis":"The normative layer."},{"question":"Must source data be deleted after an error is discovered?","answer":"If the source is reliable but the transformation is wrong, correct the layer where the error actually occurs and the decisions derived from it.","basis":"The repair layer."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/figures/d21-layers.png","alt":"Check legal sources, applicability, norms, powers, implementation, and remedies separately.","caption":"Based on D.21."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/figures/d21-time.png","alt":"Two paths with the same total yield different eligibility within a window.","caption":"All numbers are teaching constructions."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/figures/d21-remedy.png","alt":"Separate hearing, evidence correction, changing decisions, compensation, receipt, and rule revision.","caption":"Endpoints can connect without automatically proving one another."}],"diagramBrief":"Three figures distinguish legal layers, a temporal construction, and remedial endpoints.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["The full judgment and implementation records for every party were not read.","The artificial threshold demonstrates only insufficient information; it does not replace actual legal provisions."],"taskId":"S-D-21","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§D.21, historical Chinese research manuscript lines 4853–4868","claim_support":["Legal layers, powers, responsibility, information within time windows, remedial endpoints, and eight entry points."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-FCA","title":"Prygodicz v Commonwealth of Australia (No 2) [2021] FCA 634","url":"https://www.judgments.fedcourt.gov.au/judgments/Judgments/fca/single/2021/2021fca0634","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2021-06-11","locator":"2021-06-11; reasons [2], [5], and [65], and orders in Annexure C, item 1(a)–(d)","claim_support":["The judgment records the income-averaging process and the problem of variable income. Annexure C specifies that debt decisions are invalid under conditions including sole reliance on ATO data covering a longer period, without evidence of constant fortnightly income or other supporting evidence."],"limits":["The lesson author directly read the specified paragraphs and annexure, without claiming to have read the entire judgment. The example concerns only this historical case; it is not legal advice for a current individual case, and the judgment is not equated with every person actually receiving a complete remedy."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.21　法律、授权、程序与责任; Chinese source lines 5306–5321; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/source/S-D-21.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-21/diagrams.md","editionNote":"The relevant explanation in this section has been revised for v2; the original research attachments and the scope of external-source review retain their historical status.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4183a2ace6506ba7d5b16b8d205df7efd8f335dc19ec416673aa7ddbca01f3ca","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-21.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-21.md"}`),l={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-21.md"};function d(u,e,h,p,m,f){const t=n("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-21" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.21</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Law, Authorization, Procedure, and Responsibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-21" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.21 Law, Authorization, Procedure, and Responsibility {#section-d-21}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Verify legal texts, application to individual cases, powers, and actual remedies separately. Even a reliable total may be insufficient to support a decision concerning a legally critical time window.</span><details class="xk-gist__codes"><summary>Codes used in this section (1)</summary><ul><li><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-15.html#section-15-2-1"><b>T0</b>Carriers of Safety and Trust</a></li></ul></details></div><p>In the main text, see §2.4.3, §5.5.5 and 5.5.7, §11.3, §13.5, §14, and §15.2. Research entries cover 21.01–21.08.</p><p>Distinguish the validity of a source of law, its application to an individual case, moral reasons, actual authority, procedures for remedy, and what reaches people in practice. Natural law, positive law, legal realism, constitutionalism, and research on legal pluralism help analyze these questions in different ways. The existence of an institutional text does not establish implementation. A rule’s legal status does not automatically determine its normative evaluation.</p><p>Consent, authorization, agency, representation, and the right to bring a claim are distinct. Some public or statutory procedures do not depend on individual private mandates. This does not establish consent by those concerned. Personal consent also cannot substitute for the public authority an institution requires. A model’s recommendation, actual implementation, and later confirmation must be recorded separately. The fact that an observed act was unauthorized is no reason to erase it from the record.</p><p>Hearing people, respecting procedure, correcting evidence, changing a decision, paying compensation, and revising rules can occur at different times. A remedy in one case does not establish that the institution has been corrected throughout. Upholding a decision also does not necessarily mean that no one was heard. Punishment, prevention, compensation, truth, nonviolent coexistence, and forgiveness are distinct and cannot offset one another. T0 prohibits forced reconciliation. It does not rule out all lawful evidence gathering.</p><p>In a teaching example, an accurate annual income figure does not suffice to determine income in each short benefit window. Correct arithmetic cannot make up for a gap in timing information that matters under the law. Responsibilities for obtaining additional evidence, defaults in cases of absence, and suspension of implementation require specific rules. Organizational conditions can explain harm without automatically absolving individuals. An individual error also does not erase the role of upstream design, resources, and management.</p><p>Authority can pass lawfully between steps of a composite procedure. Each individual authorization must still be valid at the step where it applies. Checking each authorization separately does not mean that procedures can never connect. Several invalid fragments also cannot be assembled into valid authorization. Explanation, correcting source data, notification, changing a decision, compensation, and the person concerned receiving it freely are distinct endpoints. If the original record is reliable but the time conversion is wrong, correct the faulty inference and the decisions derived from it. There is no need to delete all reliable material.</p><p>Research topics. 21.01 Natural law, positive law, and legal realism. 21.02 Constitutionalism, separation of powers, and institutional checks and balances. 21.03 Rights, authorization, consent, and representation. 21.04 Procedural justice, evidence, and appeals. 21.05 Punishment, prevention, repair, and transitional justice. 21.06 Attribution of responsibility and organizational harm. 21.07 Legal pluralism, customary law, and transnational rules. 21.08 Algorithmic decisions, digital rights, and appealability.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.21"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=i(l,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
